Q: Use SQLite as a key:value store A: <p>Even if there exists modules that do this (see other answer), I tried to write one simple, self-contained version. Here is a class <code>KeyValueStore</code> (key and value are strings) that works like this:</p>
<h2>Demo</h2>
<pre><code>from sqlitekeyvaluestore import KeyValueStore

kv = KeyValueStore('test.db')  # uses SQLite

print(len(kv))                 # 0 item
kv['hello1'] = 'you1'
kv['hello2'] = 'you2'
kv['hello3'] = 'you3'
print(kv['hello1'])            # you1
print(len(kv))                 # 3 items

del kv['hello1']
print(len(kv))                 # 2 items remaining

print('hello1' in kv)          # False, it has just been deleted!
print('hello3' in kv)          # True

kv['hello3'] = 'newvalue'      # redefine an already present key/value
print(kv['hello3'])            # newvalue

print(kv.keys())               # ['hello2', 'hello3']
print(kv.values())             # ['you2', 'newvalue']
print(kv.items())              # [('hello2', 'you2'), ('hello3', 'newvalue')]

for k in kv:
    print(k, kv[k])

kv.close()                     # important to commit
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h2>Code: sqlitekeyvaluestore.py</h2>
<pre><code>import sqlite3

class KeyValueStore(dict):
    def __init__(self, filename=None):
        self.conn = sqlite3.connect(filename)
        self.conn.execute(&quot;CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS kv (key text unique, value text)&quot;)

    def close(self):
        self.conn.commit()
        self.conn.close()

    def __len__(self):
        rows = self.conn.execute('SELECT COUNT(*) FROM kv').fetchone()[0]
        return rows if rows is not None else 0

    def iterkeys(self):
        c = self.conn.cursor()
        for row in c.execute('SELECT key FROM kv'):
            yield row[0]

    def itervalues(self):
        c = self.conn.cursor()
        for row in c.execute('SELECT value FROM kv'):
            yield row[0]

    def iteritems(self):
        c = self.conn.cursor()
        for row in c.execute('SELECT key, value FROM kv'):
            yield row[0], row[1]

    def keys(self):
        return list(self.iterkeys())

    def values(self):
        return list(self.itervalues())

    def items(self):
        return list(self.iteritems())

    def __contains__(self, key):
        return self.conn.execute('SELECT 1 FROM kv WHERE key = ?', (key,)).fetchone() is not None

    def __getitem__(self, key):
        item = self.conn.execute('SELECT value FROM kv WHERE key = ?', (key,)).fetchone()
        if item is None:
            raise KeyError(key)
        return item[0]

    def __setitem__(self, key, value):
        self.conn.execute('REPLACE INTO kv (key, value) VALUES (?,?)', (key, value))

    def __delitem__(self, key):
        if key not in self:
            raise KeyError(key)
        self.conn.execute('DELETE FROM kv WHERE key = ?', (key,))

    def __iter__(self):
        return self.iterkeys()
</code></pre>

Q: Android 15/16: returning from browser (deeplink) causes mixed DayNight theme in fragments (new fragments use system dark, old fragments stay light) A: <p>This issue stems from a change in how Android 15 and 16 handle <strong>Configuration persistence</strong> when returning from the background via a DeepLink. The system tries to optimize performance by reusing the Activity, but it occasionally leaks the system's <code>uiMode</code> into the <code>Resources</code> object of the newly created fragments, overriding the <code>AppCompatDelegate</code> global setting.</p>
<h3>The Solution: Use <code>onConfigurationChanged</code> and <code>applyOverrideConfiguration</code></h3>
<p>To fix this reliably on Android 15+, you need to explicitly tell the Activity to ignore system theme changes and force your saved preference into the Activity's configuration.</p>
<h4>1. Update Manifest</h4>
<p>Add <code>uiMode</code> to <code>android:configChanges</code>. This prevents the system from trying to &quot;help&quot; restart your activity with system defaults when you return from the browser.</p>
<p>XML</p>
<pre><code>&lt;activity
    android:name=&quot;.MainActivity&quot;
    android:configChanges=&quot;uiMode|nightMode&quot;
    android:exported=&quot;true&quot;&gt;
    &lt;/activity&gt;
</code></pre>
<h4>2. Override <code>attachBaseContext</code></h4>
<p>By wrapping the context, you ensure that the <code>Resources</code> used by the Activity and all its Fragments are locked to your saved theme, regardless of what the OS suggests during a DeepLink resume.</p>
<p>Kotlin</p>
<pre><code>override fun attachBaseContext(newBase: Context) {
    val theme = Configs.getCurrentTheme(newBase)
    val newConfig = Configuration(newBase.resources.configuration)

    newConfig.uiMode = if (theme == &quot;dark&quot;) {
        Configuration.UI_MODE_NIGHT_YES
    } else {
        Configuration.UI_MODE_NIGHT_NO
    } or (newConfig.uiMode and Configuration.UI_MODE_NIGHT_MASK.inv())

    val context = newBase.createConfigurationContext(newConfig)
    super.attachBaseContext(context)
}
</code></pre>
<h4>3. Handle <code>onConfigurationChanged</code></h4>
<p>Since you added <code>uiMode</code> to the manifest, the system will now call this method instead of recreating the activity. You must use this to re-apply your custom theme.</p>
<p>Kotlin</p>
<pre><code>override fun onConfigurationChanged(newConfig: Configuration) {
    super.onConfigurationChanged(newConfig)
    // Force re-application of your saved theme
    initTheme() 
}
</code></pre>
